ld -- Command

Link relocatable object modules
ld [option ...] file ...

ld combines relocatable objects -- produced by cc, by as, or by an earlier
run of ld -- with the members of libraries produced by ar, and writes an
executable l.out file.  Arguments are scanned in order; an argument that is
not an option is a relocatable object or a library archive.

A library is searched only to resolve references that are still undefined
when ld reaches it, so a library named before the objects that need it
contributes nothing.

The output is made executable only when no symbol is left undefined, or
when -d was given.

Options
-d         Define common regions even when relocation information is
           retained.
-e entry   Take the next argument as the entry point.
-i         Separate instruction and data spaces.
-k[system] Link a kernel image, reading its symbols from system, or from
           /coherent when the option carries no name.
-lname     Search the library libname.a: /lib/libname.a first, then
           /usr/lib/libname.a.  ld fails if neither is there.
-L         Large model.
-m         Load in memory where possible.
-n         Shared instruction space.
-o file    Write the output to file.  The default is l.out.
-R value   Take the next argument as the relocation base.
-r         Retain relocation information in the output and issue no
           diagnostic for undefined symbols.
-s         Strip the symbol table from the output.
-u symbol  Enter symbol in the symbol table as undefined, so that a library
           member defining it is loaded.
-w         Watch: report progress as the link proceeds.
-X         Discard local compiler-generated symbols -- those whose names
           begin with `L'.
-x         Discard all local symbols.

An unrecognised option is refused with the usage message

     Usage: ld [-d] [-e entry] [-k[sysnam]] [-i] [-l<name>] [-m] [-n]
             [-o file] [-R relocbase] [-L] [-r] [-s] [-u symbol] [-X|x]
             file ...

ld also loads in memory, without -m, when the data address it has reached
is below the in-memory threshold its build sets (BREADBOX, 16384 bytes in
src/ld/run).

Files
l.out           the output file, when -o names none
/lib/lib*.a     libraries, searched first
/usr/lib/lib*.a libraries, searched second

